# Cinematography and lighting

- Choose camera height, lens, motion, and depth from the shot's story purpose. Do not force every shot to eye level, a low hero angle, or shallow depth of field.
- For 9:16, preserve the hero read, faces, gestures, feet when locomotion matters, lead room, caption-safe bands, and deliberate foreground/midground/background separation.
- Keep camera motion smooth and motivated. Respect the existing comfort ceilings; do not add handheld noise or shake by default. Small impact motion is allowed only when story-motivated, bounded, and reviewed.
- Lighting must support focal hierarchy, readable expressions, coherent sources, material response, and the approved color script. Do not force maximum saturation or warm lighting into every dramatic context.
- Reject clipping, floating dressing, repeated patterns, missing/default materials, broken reflections, incoherent shadows, empty terrain, and background competition.
- Never solve a visual defect with camera-distance material gates, per-camera shader tricks, or content hidden only from the failing view.

